## 1️⃣ Project‑wide Hardening  

| Issue | Why it matters | Fix (code snippet) |
|-------|----------------|--------------------|
| **Hard‑coded port** – `app.listen(3000)` makes it easy to run the wrong port in production and leaks implementation details. | Allows accidental exposure and hinders use of environment‑specific configurations. | ```js\nconst PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;\napp.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Server running on port ${PORT}`));\n``` |
| **Missing security headers** – No protection against click‑jacking, MIME sniffing, XSS, etc. | Attackers can manipulate browsers to run malicious code or embed the UI in an untrusted context. | ```js\nconst helmet = require('helmet');\napp.use(helmet()); // adds a robust set of security headers\n``` |
| **No CORS policy** – By default Express will reply to any `Origin`. | An open CORS policy lets any site read your JSON, which can be a data‑leak vector. | ```js\nconst cors = require('cors');\napp.use(cors({\n  origin: process.env.ALLOWED_ORIGINS?.split(',') || 'https://my‑client.example.com',\n  methods: ['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'DELETE'],\n  credentials: true,\n}));\n``` |
| **Unstructured logging** – `console.log` writes to stdout, but you lose timestamps, request IDs, and error context. | Makes debugging and audit trails harder; you can’t correlate logs with security events. | Use a logger like **pino** or **winston**:\n```js\nconst pino = require('pino');\nconst logger = pino({ level: process.env.LOG_LEVEL || 'info' });\napp.use((req, res, next) => {\n  req.log = logger.child({ reqId: req.headers['x-request-id'] || Date.now() });\n  req.log.info({ method: req.method, url: req.originalUrl });\n  next();\n});\n``` |

