## 2. Structured Error Handling

### a) Centralised error‑handling middleware
Instead of letting uncaught errors crash the process or return raw stack traces, funnel everything through a single middleware.

```js
// ----- error handling ---------------------------------------------
class ApiError extends Error {
  constructor(message, status = 500) {
    super(message);
    this.status = status;
    this.isOperational = true; // mark as expected error
    Error.captureStackTrace(this, this.constructor);
  }
}

// Example of throwing an operational error in a route
// throw new ApiError('User not found', 404);

app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  // Log the error (you could plug in winston, pino, etc.)
  console.error(err);

  // Hide internal details for non‑operational errors
  const status = err.isOperational ? err.status : 500;
  const message = err.isOperational ? err.message : 'Internal Server Error';

  res.status(status).json({ error: message });
});
```

### b) Async‑handler wrapper
Express doesn’t automatically catch rejected promises. Wrap async route handlers to forward errors to the middleware above.

```js
const asyncHandler = fn => (req, res, next) => {
  Promise.resolve(fn(req, res, next)).catch(next);
};
```

Usage:

```js
app.get(
  '/users',
  asyncHandler(async (req, res) => {
    // any async DB call goes here
    res.json([/* … */]);
  })
);
```

